00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.670
So as promised, I
was going to tell

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:14.090
you more about how the
portal was before we started.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:17.150
So at DMPM,
customers could consult

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.910
their insights of their
portfolio through the classical

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.010
BI approach.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.830
Using Qlik, they
could log in and they

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:29.350
can save their bookmarks and
filter with custom filters.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:31.430
But however,
these reports

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:34.040
were separate from
the DMPM websites

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.240
and were not customized, so
DMPM has not full ownership

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.310
of the portal.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:45.260
So DMPM wants to evolve
their portal to a serious data

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.440
science portal, and they want
to provide their customers

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.900
with richer data and insights.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:53.840
So this new portal
had some requirements

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.350
like rendering tailor
documents, including forecasts

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:01.430
on time series, still
providing classical dashboards,

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.990
but leveraging a new Azure
data platform with Power BI.

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:10.100
They want because they like
to work in a customer-oriented

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:11.750
way,

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:15.080
they want full control of the
look and feel and the user

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.540
experience, and they
also want to keep

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:18.899
ownership of the portal.

00:04:18.899 --> 00:04:23.410
And of course, the portal
needs to be future proofed.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:26.320
So R Shiny is often
thought as a dashboarding tool

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.040
and it plays the categories
like Power BI or Tableau.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.480
But R Shiny is more than that.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:35.440
Shiny can be used for
data science applications,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.310
including dashboards, full-stack
web development and real-time

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:41.680
prediction applications.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.110
As you can see from
the graph, R Shiny

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:47.440
has a very strong business
capability rating,

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.480
but the learning curve
is a bit more difficult

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:53.260
than a Power BI or a Tableau.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:57.040
Since R is an
open source language,

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.140
there are several
packages available

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:02.890
and together with DMPM we
have chosen for R Markdown,

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:05.410
Shiny, and plumber.

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:09.040
This allowed us to create
interactive web applications

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:12.850
and by using R Markdown we were
able to generate our tailor

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:15.640
documents like a PDF.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:21.330
We used plumber to separate the
logic in different functions.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:24.130
In every project you encounter
different challenges.

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:27.070
So we had some challenges on
BI and data science level,

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:29.550
but also on Azure level.

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.990
Can you combine BI
tools with data science

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:35.670
solutions? Should we
combine Power BI or Shiny

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:40.050
or redevelop in pure R?
How we can embed a Power BI

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.490
dashboard?

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:42.550
Did we manage it?

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:43.330
Yes, we did.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:47.970
But my colleague Joran DeWilde will tell
more in detail how we did it.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:49.380
Thank you, Thomas.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:53.460
Um, so when looking at the
already existing integrations

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.310
between the more
common BI tools and R,

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:59.680
we see that there are
actually a lot of options.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:03.180
Uh, first of all, it's easy
to use an R or Python script

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.930
in your data preparation.

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:07.680
Use custom visuals
within your BI tools

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.740
that originate from
R or Python, or even

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:13.800
use a plumber API
to, for instance,

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.170
embed a forecast of your data.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:21.090
So integrating R in your BI
tools isn't that difficult.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:24.600
The other way around, however,
can pose some challenges.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.600
Typically, the only
way to integrate a BI tool

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.130
into your R data
products is by

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:32.220
embedding it.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:35.010
When looking at the
integration capabilities of Tableau

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:38.370
and R, for instance, we see
two major packages,

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.500
shinytableau and
plumbertableau.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.230
Shiny tableau
being used for using

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.410
Shiny in your Tableau
dashboards and plumbertableau,

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:50.460
you can use an API
written in R in your

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:53.780
Tableau calculated fields.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:58.330
Power BI also offers quite some
R integrations.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:00.850
For instance, it's very
easy to get your data

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:04.570
using an R scripts or to
use R-powered visuals

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:06.670
in your Power BI dashboards.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:08.950
So with these two
examples, I think

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:13.720
it's quite clear that using R
in your more common BI tools

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:15.970
is quite straightforward.

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:19.260
The other way around, however,
that's another question.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:22.420
For instance, how
would you and this is also

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:26.350
the topic of the talk embed
a Power BI dashboard in your R Shiny

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:28.960
application.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:30.790
If you want to use
a dashboard that

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:33.760
was written in Power BI
in an R Shiny application,

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:37.030
the first question that arised
for us was whether or not we should.

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:42.430
We could also just migrate the
existing visuals to our R Shiny

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:46.690
application. This way, Power BI
serves more as a design tool

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:49.390
where we kind of see what
works and what doesn't, and

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:54.550
the final visuals are recreated
in our R Shiny application.

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:56.310
This is an interesting approach.

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:58.720
If you have dashboards
that don't evolve a lot,

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.180
that don't change
a lot, and you have

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:05.560
people who feel comfortable
working in R and Shiny.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:07.600
The option of embedding
is more interesting

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:09.850
if you have dashboards
that do change and evolve

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:13.810
a lot, this way you can
update your dashboards

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:17.590
without having to republish 
your app every time.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:19.390
Furthermore, you can
have a team that's

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:22.390
really dedicated to maintaining
the Power BI dashboards

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:25.390
who don't need to have
any Shiny knowledge.

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:28.540
For our case, we were definitely
in this second category

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:33.580
where we had a team of
people who didn't necessarily

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:36.429
possess the correct
amount of Shiny knowledge.

00:08:36.429 --> 00:08:41.210
And we had dashboards that
changed and evolved a lot.

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:45.700
But how does embedding a
Power BI dashboard work exactly?

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:49.220
Well, the first thing we
do is write the application

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:53.670
and the general structure of
the application itself in Shiny.

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:57.690
Next, we use our Power BI and
its drag and drop features

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:00.250
to create a nice
looking dashboard.

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:03.420
And thereafter we simply
embed the dashboard

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:06.480
via nice and clean way
into our app this way

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.130
we get a portal that, thanks
to a unified UX and style,

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:12.810
it's very difficult
for the customers

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:16.470
to notice where your Power
BI dashboard ends and the R Shiny

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:18.490
application begins.

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:22.150
Of course, to be able to
embed a Power BI dashboard,

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:24.820
you'll need a few things.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:28.540
First of all, of course, the
Power BI desktop software,

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:30.160
because with this
software you'll

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:34.480
be creating your
Power BI dashboards.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:37.510
You'll also definitely
need the Power BI Service

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:40.180
because after all, that
is where your reports

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:41.890
will be published to.

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:45.010
With the Power BI Service,
you could technically

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:48.560
embed your Power BI dashboards
already using iFrames.

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:52.390
However, if you want to embed
it using the Power BI API,

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:55.180
you'll also need Power
BI Dedicated Capacity,

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:59.050
which you can either get via
Power BI Premium or Power BI

00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:00.310
Embedded.

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:02.470
Keep in mind, however,
that this option will

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:05.710
be a bit more pricey, but
I hope to convince you

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:08.020
in the next slide why
this extra cost actually

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:10.410
might be worth it.

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:14.280
So the easiest solution
is using iFrames

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:16.270
to embed your Power BI dashboard.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:19.740
However, this comes with
some very big disadvantages.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:22.170
First, you'll have
to authenticate again

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:25.020
in each iFrame, which means
that the customer will

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:28.650
have to log in for each
dashboard that it wants to see.

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:30.720
And there are also no
interactions possible

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:34.710
between your Power BI dashboard
and your Shiny app.

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:39.480
These disadvantages are
negated by using API embedding,

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:43.530
because thanks 
to the Power BI API,

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:47.070
you can integrate an automatic
authentication.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:50.430
And with the API, you can also
catch all the interactions,

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:53.220
which means that you can
actually have your R Shiny

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:57.120
app communicate with your
Power BI dashboard, this way

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:00.750
you can create bookmarks,
apply and delete filters,

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:04.150
and, and many, many,
many more options.

00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:07.000
But how does one embed
a Power BI dashboard

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:08.430
into an R Shiny app,

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:09.320
you may ask?

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:11.680
Well, fortunately
for us, Microsoft

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:15.910
offers a lot of examples on
how to embed a Power BI dashboard

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:17.920
using the API.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:21.580
Unfortunately, however,
there is no clear example

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:23.390
of how to do it using

00:11:23.390 --> 00:11:25.600
R Shiny.

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:28.240
Lucky for us, and probably
unlucky for others,

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.440
there's JavaScript to the rescue
via the Power BI JavaScript

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:33.050
library.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:37.810
You can actually embed a Power
BI dashboard using JavaScript.

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:39.460
This means that
your R Shiny app

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:41.320
and the JavaScript
files will have

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:43.870
to communicate with each other.

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:47.500
On the R Shiny website, there's
already a very nice overview

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.380
of how to set up the
interactions between your R

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:52.390
and your JavaScript.

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:53.660
A brief overview.

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:58.030
So, sendCustomMessage can
be used in your R application

00:11:58.030 --> 00:12:01.870
to trigger JavaScript
functions or JavaScript code.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:03.910
These triggers can
be read and code

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:06.550
using the addCustomMessageHandler 
in your JavaScript

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:11.410
files and to send input
back from your JavaScript

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:13.070
to your Shiny application,

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:17.320
The Shiny.onInputChange
button can be used.

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:21.790
A quick example of how we use
these functions in our app.

00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:24.850
So when a user navigates
in your R Shiny app

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:28.240
to a page where there should
be a Power BI dashboard,

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:30.400
we will call the
sendCustomMessage

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:34.840
function, which will call the
EmbedApi function in your JavaScript

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:35.890
files.

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:38.230
With this custom message
we'll also send some

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:40.990
parameters for our report.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:46.180
Next the powerbi-embed.js
JavaScript file,

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:50.740
the addCustomMessageHandler
will catch this callEmbedApi

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:55.030
and using some magic JavaScript
code

00:12:55.030 --> 00:12:57.610
and using the
library, we can

00:12:57.610 --> 00:12:59.830
embed the Power BI
dashboards

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:03.340
at a specific container
in our R Shiny app.

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:04.810
When it's done
rendering, we will

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:06.610
let our app know
that a report

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:10.990
has been rendered by using
the setInputValue and our

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:14.110
R Shiny app can continue
with its next step or steps

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:17.140
in the process.

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:20.710
To realize the authentication
and the more complex

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:24.550
interactions, some more complex
embedding steps were needed.

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:27.700
We won't go into too much
detail in this talk,

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:30.670
but I can share with you
already the tip of the embedding

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:31.930
iceberg.

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:35.370
For authentication, we
use the AzureAuth package.

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:38.170
This together with
the httr package

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:40.990
for retrieving the
embed token, made

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:44.620
it possible to integrate the
anticipation within our Shiny

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:45.190
app.

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:47.740
So no more two
different

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:50.110
authentication is necessary.

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:54.310
The Power BI API
was then

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:58.420
used to create bookmarks
to apply and delete filters

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:01.530
and so on and so on.

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:05.070
I want to conclude with
a brief overview of how

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:09.510
to embed a Power BI dashboard
into a Shiny app. For everyone

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:11.010
just tuning in,
this is probably one

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:14.040
of the most important
slides of our talk.

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:18.180
So we start by getting an
access token via Azure.

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:21.600
With this access token,
we can get the embed URL

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:24.540
from the Power BI API.

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.390
And with this URL together
with Javascript,

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:32.530
we can render our Power BI reports
in a container in our app.

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:36.370
Keep in mind, however, that
you will need Power BI Embedded

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:39.080
Capacity for this to work.

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:41.750
Now for the closing
thoughts of this talk,

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:43.130
I want to give
the spotlight back

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:45.940
to my colleague, Thomas.

00:14:45.940 --> 00:14:48.490
So what lessons have
we learned today?

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:51.100
Align your Power BI
style and your layout

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:52.900
within your Shiny app.

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:56.110
If you can't see where the Shiny
ends and

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:57.790
your Power BI begins,

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:00.010
you are doing it right.

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:02.470
Be mindful and aware
of some cloud costs

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:04.540
and add logging where you can.

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:08.930
Shiny can log Power BI
actions as well.

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:12.170
So at the start of the
presentation, I mentioned this,

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:13.770
those were the check marks.

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:16.610
But did we cover them all?

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:19.670
We covered some of
debunking choices.

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:23.450
We have seen some examples
of Shiny in production.

00:15:23.450 --> 00:15:27.110
You can
find our code samples

00:15:27.110 --> 00:15:29.660
via the link on the
slide where

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:32.390
you can find step by
step documents

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:34.520
created with Quarto.

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:38.330
And, but we didn't cover
a reference to a famous

00:15:38.330 --> 00:15:39.610
man in a bow tie.

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:43.060
So, without further
ado, that's all, folks.

00:15:45.790 --> 00:15:46.290
Thank you.

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:50.560
All right.

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:52.080
Thank you, everybody.

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:54.815
Any questions from the audience?

00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:00.090
If not, I will
read off of Slido.

00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:04.570
All right.

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:07.030
For Thomas and
Joran, why did you

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:10.330
decide to integrate
BI for the portal

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:14.130
into Shiny instead of the
other way around? At my org,

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:18.300
the audience for BI is bigger
than R!

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:28.160
So we think
that, with using

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:30.680
R Shiny, you have a lot
more options than just staying

00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:35.270
in Power BI, which is why
we chose to actually do it

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:35.940
this way.

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:38.840
It's also a very
interesting concept

00:16:38.840 --> 00:16:40.340
to embed a Power BI
dashboard in

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:43.220
Shiny app.
And Shiny offers way

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:46.080
more options and customizability
than Power BI.

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:49.350
You're not stuck in
the logic of Power BI.

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:52.550
You have
more freedom

00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:54.420
which is also what
the customer wanted.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:57.535
So we, we chose for
this way of working.

00:17:01.730 --> 00:17:03.840
OK, we have time for more.

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:10.349
So, how did you
ensure both Power BI and Shiny

00:17:10.349 --> 00:17:11.630
use the same data?

00:17:20.079 --> 00:17:23.079
That's a very
interesting question,

00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:27.099
which I don't have
the answer to right now.

00:17:27.099 --> 00:17:29.660
But yeah, definitely

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:31.560
come get us at our booth.

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:33.435
And we can discuss
this in finer detail.

00:17:37.650 --> 00:17:39.520
Any questions from the audience?

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:40.760
I've got one more on Slido.

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:46.850
All right.

00:17:46.850 --> 00:17:50.660
Do you know if other BI
tools like Tableau

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:56.690
have API-driven authentication
like Power BI does?

00:17:56.690 --> 00:17:58.280
Could you repeat the question?

00:17:58.280 --> 00:18:04.700
Absolutely. Do you know if other
BI tools like Tableau

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:08.935
have API-driven authentication
like Power BI does?

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:18.240
So, we believe that the same
setup could actually also

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:20.700
be achieved
with Tableau.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:23.550
Tableau also has an API for embedding.

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:26.100
However, we chose for
Power BI in this case

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:28.800
because our customer was
already heavily relying

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:32.160
on the Microsoft stack, which
made it an obvious choice

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:35.200
to choose Power BI to embed.

00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:39.470
But it is also possible
with the Tableau API.

